  "grammar": {
    "title": "Obrigado or obrigada? And how to say \"you\"",
    "body": [
      "\"Obrigado\" literally means \"obliged\", so it matches the person speaking: a man says \"obrigado\", a woman says \"obrigada\". It doesn't depend on who you're thanking.",
      "Portuguese has several ways to say \"you\". Use \"tu\" with friends, people your age, kids and in the surf lineup. The verb ends in -s: \"Podes repetir?\"",
      "With strangers, older people and shop staff, be polite: leave out the word for \"you\" and use the verb without -s: \"Pode repetir?\" To be extra polite, add \"o senhor\" (to a man) or \"a senhora\" (to a woman).",
      "Tip: when unsure, use the polite form without a pronoun. You'll never sound rude."
    ],
    "examples": [
      { "id": "u0-g01", "pt": "Obrigado!", "en": "Thank you! (said by a man)" },
      { "id": "u0-g02", "pt": "Obrigada!", "en": "Thank you! (said by a woman)" },
      { "id": "u0-g03", "pt": "Podes repetir?", "en": "Can you repeat? (to a friend)" },
      { "id": "u0-g04", "pt": "Pode repetir?", "en": "Can you repeat? (polite)" },
      { "id": "u0-g05", "pt": "O senhor fala inglês?", "en": "Do you speak English, sir?" }
    ],
    "epNote": "In Brazil \"você\" is the normal word for \"you\". In Portugal it can sound distant or even rude, so skip it: use \"tu\" with friends, and the polite verb form without a pronoun with everyone else."
  },
